Oh yeah, good point.

In fact that might be a reason why *not* making a separate flag is better, i.e., depending on how smart the trimmer is about static analysis. For example, whether:

```cs
if (someValueThatTheTrimmerWillEvaluateAsFalse)
{
   // ...
}
```

and 

```cs
var shouldDoStuff = someValueThatTheTrimmerWillEvaluateAsFalse;
if (shouldDoStuff)
{
   // ...
}
```

... are actually equivalent, or whether the first is better because the entire `if(false)` is eliminated. I see that the earlier version of this code by Pranav did inline the trimmer-replacable value into the `if` test. Further, I don't know how smart the interpreter is about detecting and eliding `if(false)` blocks during its MSIL->interp-instructions pass.

In this case it's probably not something to worry about, just an interesting thought.
